<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2">
<Document>
  <name>Wednesday Tokyo → Kyoto · May 27</name>
  <description><![CDATA[07:00 Shinkansen · Nishiki + Ippodo · Ritz check-in 15:00 · Mizuki/Pontocho dinner]]></description>

  <Style id="locked"><IconStyle><scale>1.3</scale><Icon><href>https://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/paddle/ylw-stars.png</href></Icon></IconStyle></Style>
  <Style id="major"><IconStyle><scale>1.1</scale><Icon><href>https://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/paddle/red-circle.png</href></Icon></IconStyle></Style>
  <Style id="bonus"><IconStyle><scale>0.9</scale><Icon><href>https://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/paddle/blu-blank.png</href></Icon></IconStyle></Style>
  <Style id="station"><IconStyle><scale>0.9</scale><Icon><href>https://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/shapes/rail.png</href></Icon></IconStyle></Style>

  <Folder>
    <name>LOCKED — confirmed bookings / hard anchors</name>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Shinkansen Nozomi 117 (Tokyo → Kyoto)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>BOARD 06:50 · DEPART 07:00 · ARRIVE Kyoto 09:12</b><br><br><b>BOOKING:</b> ✅ CONFIRMED · Reservation 2005 · Green Car · Car 10, Seats 12-C (aisle) + 12-D (window) · D side is Fuji side for Tokyo→Kyoto · Fuji window ~07:35-08:00<br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Nozomi 117 N700 16-car set, Tokyo Station → Kyoto direct (~2 hr 12 min). Green Car has 2+2 seating with attendant service, hot oshibori, power outlets, in-car Wi-Fi (login: shinkansen-free-wifi).<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> 12-D is the window seat on the Mt. Fuji side. ~07:35-08:00 between Shin-Yokohama and Shizuoka is the visibility window — weather-pending, 70-80% Fuji visibility this time of year on clear mornings.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Premier Tokaido Shinkansen service; 'Nozomi' = fastest stopping pattern, 'N700' is the current rolling stock with Green Car amenities and seat-rotation at terminals so you always face forward.<br><br>Address: Tokyo Station Tokaido Shinkansen platforms (south end of station)<br><br><b>↪ From hotel: leave 06:00 sharp · Yurikamome to Shimbashi 1 stop OR walk to Hamamatsuchō 8 min then Yamanote to Tokyo 5 min · arrive Tokyo Station no later than 06:40</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#locked</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7671,35.6812,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Ritz-Carlton Kyoto (HOTEL)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Check-in 15:00 nominal · Your24™ flex via Ambassador</b><br><br><b>BOOKING:</b> ✅ CONFIRMED · Confirmation 90261043 · Corner Suite MINAMI · King · River view · 142,000 JPY total (152k points + cash upgrade) · cancel deadline passed (May 24 23:59) · Ambassador Elite<br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Ritz-Carlton Kyoto on the Kamogawa river at Nijo-Ohashi bridge. Kerry Hill Architects building, low-rise, river-facing rooms. Address Kamogawa Nijo-Ohashi Hotori, Nakagyo-ku.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> Walking distance to Pontocho (5 min), Nishiki Market (10 min), Gion (15 min), Ippodo Tea (10 min). Mizuki (Michelin 1-star sushi) is in the hotel. Ambassador Elite Your24 means you can request 12-13:00 early check-in by messaging your Ambassador 48 hrs ahead.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Mizuki Sushi (11m Wajima-lacquer 10-seat counter, Michelin 1-star 6 years running) is in the hotel; Spa Karya is the in-house spa; the Pierre Hermé pâtisserie is here.<br><br>Address: Kamogawa Nijo-Ohashi Hotori, Nakagyo-ku, Kyoto 604-0902 · +81-75-7465555<br><br><b>↪ From Kyoto Station: taxi 15 min, ¥1,800-2,400 (beats subway with bags)</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#locked</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>135.7707,35.0163,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
  </Folder>

  <Folder>
    <name>MAJOR — the planned spine</name>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Kyoto Station arrival → Ritz bag drop</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>09:12 arrive · 09:30 taxi · 09:50 bags dropped</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Kyoto Station Hachijo exit south side for taxi rank. Skip the subway (Karasuma → Tozai transfer + 15 min walk with bags).<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> 15-min taxi ride to Ritz, ¥1,800-2,400. Drop bags with bellhop, message Ambassador about Your24 early-check, head out for Nishiki.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Kyoto Station Hachijo (south) entrance has the bigger taxi rank; Karasuma (north) entrance is busier but has the subway lines.<br><br><b>↪ Off Shinkansen, follow signs to Hachijo exit, taxi rank is at street level</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#major</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>135.7588,34.9858,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Nishiki Market (Karolina pick)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>10:00-11:30 · ~90 min · mid-morning beats the crush</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> 400-year-old covered market, 5 blocks long, ~100 vendors selling Kyoto specialty foods: yuba (soy-milk skim), tsukemono pickles, fresh tofu, dashi, octopus skewers, wagashi, knives, tea. Western end is the calm end.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> The Karolina-shaped Kyoto morning. Slow browse, sample as you walk, buy pickles to carry home. Quintessential Kyoto food culture.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Tanaka Keiran for dashimaki tamago (90-year recipe), Sawawa for Uji matcha sweets, Konnamonja for hot soy-milk doughnuts, Nishiki Soya at western end for fresh yuba, Aritsugu for knives (already covered by Kappabashi — skip).<br><br>Address: Nishiki Koji St, Nakagyo Ward · runs east-west between Teramachi and Takakura streets<br><br><b>↪ From Ritz: 10 min walk south along Teramachi</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#major</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>135.7647,35.005,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Weekenders Coffee Tominokoji</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>11:45-12:15 · daily 7:30-18 · stand + outdoor seats</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Kyoto specialty coffee tucked behind a parking lot in a converted machiya — lighter Scandinavian-style roast, single-origin focus. Founded by Kyoto's most respected coffee roaster.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> The local-craft coffee pick. Closer + better than the % Arabica line at Higashiyama. The machiya setting and the 'hidden behind a parking lot' approach is the Kyoto-coffee aesthetic.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Kyoto's most-respected indie roaster; the parking-lot-hidden machiya setting; lighter roast preference distinguishes from Tokyo's deeper-roast scene.<br><br><b>↪ 5 min from Nishiki Market</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#major</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>135.7651,35.0058,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Ippodo Tea Kaboku Tearoom</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>12:30-13:30 · daily 10-17 (last call 16:30) · closed 2nd Wed monthly (May 27 = 4th Wed = OPEN)</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Tea house at Ippodo's Kyoto main store on Teramachi Street. Founded 1717. 44-seat tearoom in three areas where you have tea prepared for you OR self-prepare with staff guidance. NOT a performative ceremony — the real Kyoto tea-house experience.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> The authentic Kyoto answer to the Sakurai-Tokyo rejection. No performative ceremony, just craft. Lineage to 1717. Walk-in.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Founded 1717; 308-year continuous family operation; the canonical Kyoto tea house; the matcha and sencha sold here are the reference standard.<br><br>Address: 52 Tokiwagi-cho, Nakagyo-ku<br><br><b>↪ 10 min walk back toward Ritz on Teramachi Street</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#major</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>135.7677,35.0153,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Teramachi-Sanjo arcade (afternoon walk back)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>13:30-14:30 · covered shopping arcade</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Long covered shopping arcade running north-south through central Kyoto. Mix of old-Kyoto stationery, ceramics galleries, tea shops, antiques, and modern Japanese fashion.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> Covered (good for any weather), full of one-of-a-kind small shops. Natural walking route between Ippodo and Ritz. Browse pace.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Centuries-old shopping street; the covered arcade format is canonical Kyoto-central; mix of generations of shops.<br><br><b>↪ Runs along Teramachi, north toward Ritz</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#major</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>135.7674,35.009,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Mizuki Sushi (Ritz · Michelin 1*)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Wed DINNER candidate · 19:00 or 19:30 · BOOK via Ambassador</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> 10-seat counter inside the Ritz-Carlton Kyoto. 11m single Wajima-lacquer slab. Edo-style sushi by chef Hiroshi Mizuki. Michelin 1-star six consecutive years.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> Pure Edo-style sushi, counter intimacy, no fusion — exactly the pattern. Plus it's IN your hotel, which means no transit and you can extend your wine/sake intake without worrying about getting home. Ambassador can lift the reservation if not already booked.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Michelin 1-star sushi inside a Ritz-Carlton (rare combination); the Wajima-lacquer counter as installation art; Edo-style tradition.<br><br>Address: Ritz-Carlton Kyoto, ground floor]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#major</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>135.7707,35.0163,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
  </Folder>

  <Folder>
    <name>BONUS — discovery zone</name>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Pontocho Sushi Ishiya (dinner alt)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Wed dinner alt · 18:00 or 20:00 · book via Ambassador</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Hidden sushi counter in the Pontocho alley itself. Seasonal courses before sushi. Reasonable pricing for the caliber.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> If Mizuki is booked, Ishiya is the Pontocho-alley pick — the location IS the experience, lantern-lit narrow alley.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Pontocho-alley setting; seasonal multi-course before sushi; one of Pontocho's hidden serious counters.<br><br><b>↪ 10 min walk from Ritz along Kamogawa to Pontocho</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>135.771,35.0078,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Sushi Matsumoto</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Wed dinner alt · Michelin 1* · counter omakase</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Offshoot of the Tokyo Akasaka original. Michelin 1-star. Counter omakase, lunch omakase as a value option.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> Tokyo-pedigree sushi in Kyoto. Counter omakase. Michelin caliber without the Mizuki price tag.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Tokyo Akasaka lineage; Michelin 1-star Kyoto; counter omakase with lunch-tier value option.]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>135.7705,35.0028,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Sumiyaki Sosai Toriya Hitomi (yakitori)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Wed dinner alt · 'hardest yakitori reservation in Kyoto' · 30+ days lead time</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Sumiyaki Sosai Toriya Hitomi on Nijo Street, close to Ritz. Charcoal yakitori focused on heritage chicken.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> If you wanted yakitori in Kyoto, this is THE one — but it books out 30+ days. Try via byFood or AutoReserve as a long-shot.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Most-difficult yakitori reservation in Kyoto; charcoal-grilled heritage chicken; near Ritz.<br><br><b>↪ Walking distance from Ritz on Nijo Street</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>135.7691,35.0142,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Pontocho Alley (post-dinner walk)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Post-dinner · lantern-lit alley · ~20 min stroll</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Narrow lantern-lit pedestrian alley running parallel to the Kamogawa river, lined with traditional restaurants and bars. Iconic Kyoto evening stroll.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> The canonical post-dinner Kyoto walk. Wooden machiya buildings, paper lanterns, glimpses of the river through side openings.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Kyoto's iconic dining alley; geisha occasionally cross between teahouses; the architecture is preserved Edo-era machiya.<br><br><b>↪ 5 min walk from Ritz along Kamogawa</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>135.7715,35.0078,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Bee's Knees Kiyamachi (cocktail)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Post-dinner · ~21:00 · Asia's 50 Best Bars 4×</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Hidden cocktail bar behind a yellow door labeled 'Book Store' on Kiyamachi Street. Asia's 50 Best Bars list for four years running.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> Top-tier cocktails in a deliberately hidden setting. The 'find the unmarked door' aesthetic is the Kyoto bar play.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Asia's 50 Best Bars × 4; the yellow Book Store door; the Kiyamachi location.<br><br><b>↪ 8 min walk from Ritz</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>135.7703,35.0068,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Bar Rocking Chair</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Post-dinner alt · Kenji Tsubokura's bar</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Kenji Tsubokura's cocktail bar, focused on Japanese-ingredient cocktails (Japanese spirits, yuzu, shiso, hojicha, etc.).<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> Cocktail thread + Japanese-ingredient focus. Tsubokura is one of Japan's most-cited bartenders for indigenous-ingredient cocktail innovation.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Japanese-ingredient cocktail focus; Tsubokura's craft reputation; understated Kyoto setting.]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>135.7672,35.0058,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>L'Escamoteur (Pontocho speakeasy)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Post-dinner alt · speakeasy · illusionist-bartender</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Pontocho speakeasy where the lead bartender doubles as a magician/illusionist — drinks come with sleight-of-hand presentations.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> Theatrical end-of-night option. Different register from Bee's Knees craft-purity vibe.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Magician-bartender presentation; Pontocho hidden setting; theatrical cocktail experience.<br><br><b>↪ 10 min walk from Ritz to Pontocho</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>135.7713,35.0082,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Asahido Honten (Kiyomizuyaki ceramics)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Higashiyama detour · daily 9-18</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Canonical Kiyomizuyaki pottery shop at the gate of Kiyomizu-dera temple. Founded 1870. The most authoritative Kiyomizu-ware shop in Kyoto.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> If you want one piece of Kiyomizuyaki to take home, this is the shop. The 1870 lineage matters here.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> 1870 founding; gate-of-Kiyomizu-dera location; the reference Kiyomizuyaki shop in Kyoto.<br><br><b>↪ Higashiyama detour — only do if Kiyomizu-dera is on the agenda</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>135.7841,34.9952,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>% Arabica Kyoto Higashiyama (skip unless...)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Skip unless Yasaka Pagoda photo matters</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> % Arabica's Higashiyama flagship with the Yasaka Pagoda framed in the background — the most-Instagrammed Kyoto coffee shot.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> 20-min cross-town hike with a line. Skip unless Karolina specifically wants the pagoda backdrop photo. Weekenders is better as a coffee stop.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> The Yasaka Pagoda backdrop; long queue most days; the flagship of % Arabica's brand.]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>135.7806,34.9966,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
  </Folder>

  <Folder>
    <name>STATIONS — transit hubs</name>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Tokyo Station (Tokaido Shinkansen)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Depart 07:00 · arrive by 06:40</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Tokaido Shinkansen platforms are at the south end of Tokyo Station. From the JR Yamanote concourse, follow signs to 'Tokaido Shinkansen' / '東海道新幹線'.]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#station</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7671,35.6812,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Kyoto Station (Hachijo exit south)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Arrive 09:12 · taxi rank south side</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Shinkansen platforms at the Hachijo (south) side. Taxi rank at Hachijo exit. To Ritz: 15 min, ¥1,800-2,400.]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#station</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>135.7588,34.9858,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
  </Folder>

</Document>
</kml>
